[{:seon.task/name "root-evals-through-the-one-repl"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: \"agents ... connect to whatever repl server they need and for it to execute as faithfully as we can so they see what the seon agent sees.\" An MCP eval into an agent already goes through that agent's own REPL handler (middleware, the one printer, recorded :outside). An eval as root does not: seon.entrance/root-eval (entrance.clj:32-56) is a second REPL with plain read/eval/pr-str, intern of `sys`, no printer budget, no cost, no error map. Root's evals go through seon.repl/handler with a root session (sys in the session, *1 *e kept), printed by seon.print, recorded on main with origin :outside, like an agent's."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.entrance-test/a-root-eval-replies-like-an-agent-eval}}
 {:seon.task/name "inspect-a-kept-run"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: \"Can we restore a session and replay repl commands and then an agent can debug a mostly faithful re-creation?\" Runs are kept (target/check/<time>/runs, next/runs, t5/out/*.store). A root opens a kept store for inspection: its reconciler observes and acts on nothing (no spawn, no kill, no judge) unless released, so a run's main and branches can be queried and its agents restored without resuming its work. bin/root --inspect STORE; the MCP tools work against it."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.entrance-test/an-inspected-run-starts-nothing}}
 {:seon.task/name "restore-and-replay-an-agent"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: \"this ability to restore sessions and have agents go over the work with new instructions is huge. We can branch from any point right?\" Proved 2026-10-21 on t5/out/real3.store (a copy): Datahike keeps every commit as an immutable value with its parents (datahike.versioning/branch-history; branch! accepts a commit id; commit-as-db loads one); task-t5-edit-forms has 19 commits over 335 evals, and d/branch! from the middle commit made a branch holding exactly its 29 evals. So: branch from any commit at no copy cost, and for any eval between commits, branch from the commit before it and replay the rest. Restore: an agent forked on a kept branch at its end state (resume's load of the branch program). Replay to step N: an agent forked from the attempt's start (its basis), its recorded forms 1..N evaluated in order through its own REPL, every effect answered from its recorded outcome by a replay owner (nothing fires); each form's result compared with its record, a divergence recorded (nondeterminism, or a gap in the record). The debugging agent then works the re-creation through seon_eval, with new instructions as a task whose :evidence names the source run, branch and point. Needs every form's exact text (seon.repl/eval-record-keeps-a-failing-form-whole, widened to every form) and effect outcomes recorded whole (the model's reply text is on the turn; file and other effects' answers are not)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.entrance/inspect-a-kept-run" "seon.repl/eval-record-keeps-a-failing-form-whole"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.entrance-test/a-replayed-agent-reproduces-its-recorded-results
                      seon.entrance-test/a-divergence-on-replay-is-recorded}}
 {:seon.task/name "reading-the-context-is-recorded"
  :seon.task/plan "Owner: \"how can we record this (don't code up a shitload of code around a harness, just make it so this is easy to do in whatever form we end up in)\". An outside agent working a task through the entrance (attach, context, eval, done) is already recorded by the system: the attempt on main, each eval on the agent's branch with origin :outside, the judgment at done. The one step missing is reading the context: seon.entrance's :context op returns the current-state block and records nothing. Record it as a turn on the agent's branch (origin :outside, the exact text returned), the same shape as a model turn, so an outside session reads, replays and mines exactly like a Seon agent's attempt. A few lines in the entrance; no harness."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.entrance-test/a-context-read-is-a-recorded-turn}}]
